-- Q3946: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 402(b) and Sec. 83(a), a secular trust protects assets from the employer''s creditors, creating a secured beneficial interest. Because funds are no longer at substantial risk of forfeiture, the executive is taxed immediately when contributions vest, unlike the deferred taxation of a rabbi trust.
Wrong (A): Secular trust contributions are taxable to the executive when vested, not tax-free. The employer may receive a deduction, but the executive faces immediate taxation.
Wrong (B): There is a significant tax difference. Rabbi trust assets remain at creditor risk, deferring taxation, while secular trust assets are protected, triggering immediate taxation.
Wrong (C): Secular trust distributions are ordinary income, not capital gains. The income character follows Sec. 83(a) compensation treatment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3946;

-- Q3947: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 1222(3), the $18 per share post-exercise gain ($60 - $42) is long-term capital gain because the shares were held for more than one year after exercise. The employee''s basis became $42 (FMV at exercise) under Sec. 1012, and the two-year holding period satisfies the long-term requirement.
Wrong (A): The $18 gain from exercise to sale is not ordinary income. The ordinary income component ($30 spread) was already recognized at exercise under Sec. 83(a).
Wrong (B): Short-term capital gain requires a holding period of one year or less. Since the shares were held for two years after exercise, the gain is long-term.
Wrong (D): Post-exercise gain on NSO shares is capital gain, not tax-exempt. No provision exempts this appreciation from taxation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3947;

-- Q3948: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 132(f), qualified transportation fringe benefits include qualified parking at or near the employer''s business premises. Employer-provided parking at a commercial lot near the office qualifies for exclusion up to the monthly limit.
Wrong (B): Gasoline reimbursements for personal commuting are not a qualified transportation fringe benefit under Sec. 132(f) and are taxable.
Wrong (C): Employer-paid tolls for driving to work are not listed as a qualified transportation fringe benefit and are taxable compensation.
Wrong (D): A company car used exclusively for commuting does not qualify under Sec. 132(f). Qualified benefits are limited to parking, transit passes, and vanpooling.$EXPL$
