-- Q3949: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 83(b), the founder includes $50,000 (100,000 x $0.50) in ordinary income at grant. All future appreciation above $0.50 per share is taxed as capital gain, which qualifies as long-term if held more than one year. For a high-growth startup, this converts potentially large ordinary income at vesting into lower-taxed capital gains.
Wrong (A): The founder does not avoid all income tax. The 83(b) election accelerates $50,000 of ordinary income recognition to the grant date.
Wrong (C): A Sec. 83(b) election accelerates taxation to the grant date rather than deferring it. Without the election, taxation occurs at vesting.
Wrong (D): The stock value is not deductible by the founder as a business expense. The $50,000 is income to the founder, not a deduction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3949;

-- Q3950: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 409A, newly eligible participants may make a deferral election within 30 days of initial eligibility. The election applies only to compensation earned after the election date, not retroactively to compensation already earned. This is an exception to the general prior-year election rule.
Wrong (B): A new employee hired mid-year cannot elect before January 1 of their hire year because they were not yet eligible. The 30-day new-eligibility rule applies instead.
Wrong (C): The 60-day window from the first payroll date is not a recognized timeframe under Section 409A deferral election rules.
Wrong (D): Deferral elections cannot be made at any time during the first year. Section 409A strictly limits the window to 30 days from initial eligibility.$EXPL$
