-- Q3955: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 422(a), ISOs provide that no regular federal income tax is due at exercise if the employee satisfies the statutory holding periods — two years from grant and one year from exercise. All gain is deferred to the disposition date. The spread at exercise is an AMT preference item under Sec. 56(b)(3).
Wrong (A): The employer does not receive a deduction at ISO exercise. Under Sec. 421(a)(2), the employer gets no deduction for qualifying ISO dispositions. Employer deductions apply to NSOs under Sec. 83(h).
Wrong (C): ISOs can only be granted to employees under Sec. 422(a)(1). Independent contractors are not eligible for ISOs.
Wrong (D): ISOs do have an annual limit. Under Sec. 422(d), the $100,000 annual cap limits the FMV of stock first exercisable in any calendar year.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3955;

-- Q3956: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 409A and Rev. Proc. 92-64, rabbi trust assets remain subject to the employer''s general creditors in bankruptcy. The executive becomes an unsecured general creditor with no priority. This creditor risk is the trade-off for tax deferral under Sec. 451.
Wrong (A): Deferred amounts are not fully protected. The defining feature of a rabbi trust is that assets are available to the employer''s general creditors upon insolvency.
Wrong (B): The executive does not receive priority in bankruptcy. Rabbi trust participants are general unsecured creditors with the same standing as other creditors.
Wrong (C): A rabbi trust does not automatically convert to a secular trust upon bankruptcy. The trust terms and creditor exposure remain unchanged.$EXPL$
